-      "original_required_outcome": "- A declared process-architecture scope identifying the organization, system, value stream, product, service, capability, population, environment, jurisdiction, lifecycle horizon, and authority boundary covered.\n- A process taxonomy and catalog with stable identities, purposes, outcomes, triggers, owners, participants, boundaries, variants, states, dependencies, controls, evidence, measures, and lifecycle status.\n- A value-stream-to-process view connecting desired outcomes and stakeholder journeys to capabilities, processes, functions, information, decisions, products, services, and evidence.\n- Current-state process evidence that distinguishes observed behavior, documented procedure, policy expectation, informal workaround, proposed design, and unknown condition.\n- A target-state process architecture that preserves approved requirements, domain meaning, risk treatments, mandatory constraints, accessibility, security, privacy, safety, reliability, resilience, and owner-control obligations.\n- Process models showing start conditions, end conditions, activities, decisions, parallel work, synchronization, handoffs, waits, queues, exceptions, escalations, controls, evidence capture, and recovery paths as applicable.\n- Explicit process ownership and decision rights for each material step, including who may initiate, approve, execute, observe, verify, override, suspend, resume, or retire a process.\n- Process-to-function, process-to-capability, process-to-requirement, process-to-control, process-to-data, process-to-system, process-to-role, process-to-test, and process-to-evidence traceability.\n- A process variant model for environments, jurisdictions, user groups, operating modes, products, data classes, risk classes, and lifecycle stages without uncontrolled copying or drift.\n- A process performance and assurance model distinguishing activity completion, throughput, latency, quality, outcome, control effectiveness, user impact, resilience, recovery, and evidence freshness.\n- A process change, exception, escalation, rollback, migration, deprecation, and retirement model that can be handed to Functional Architecture, Operational Architecture, Information Architecture, delivery, testing, operations, maintenance, and lifecycle governance.",
-      "original_applicability": "- A capability, value stream, product, service, system, organization, or human-AI workflow requires multiple activities, actors, decisions, handoffs, states, controls, or lifecycle stages.\n- Work crosses organizational, system, domain, supplier, partner, jurisdictional, physical, or data boundaries.\n- Current work includes undocumented manual steps, queues, workarounds, approvals, escalations, exceptions, or shadow systems.\n- A change may alter who performs work, which decisions are made, what evidence is created, how users or operators recover, or where authority is exercised.\n- The process carries material security, privacy, safety, accessibility, legal, financial, ethical, reliability, resilience, support, maintenance, or retirement consequences.\n- Automation, AI, agents, orchestration, or workflow tools are proposed to recommend, decide, route, transform, approve, execute, monitor, or recover work.\n- Requirements or architecture must be allocated to end-to-end journeys and accountable process steps rather than to abstract components alone.\n- Performance, quality, control effectiveness, user outcome, operational readiness, or benefit cannot be understood from component metrics alone.",
-      "original_non_applicability": null,
