-      "original_required_outcome": "- A declared functional-architecture scope identifying the system or organizational boundary, objectives, environments, users, operators, affected parties, lifecycle horizon, and authority.\n- A stable functional taxonomy and hierarchy with clear parent-child meaning, function identity, purpose, inputs, outputs, conditions, constraints, owner, allocation, dependencies, evidence, and lifecycle state.\n- A functional context model showing external actors, systems, services, people, devices, data, events, resources, commands, controls, authorities, trust boundaries, and evidence flows.\n- A functional decomposition that covers normal, boundary, denied, degraded, failure, recovery, maintenance, migration, deprecation, and retirement behavior where applicable.\n- Functional flows and behavior models connecting triggers, preconditions, transformations, decisions, outputs, states, timing, sequencing, concurrency, exceptions, and postconditions.\n- Explicit allocation of functions and interfaces to human roles, AI roles, system elements, components, services, devices, organizations, external systems, processes, or enabling systems.\n- Functional contracts defining inputs, outputs, units, schemas, identity, authority, timing, ordering, quality, error behavior, retry, idempotency, audit, evidence, and compatibility as applicable.\n- Traceability from objectives, domain meaning, requirements, capabilities, processes, risks, controls, functions, allocations, implementations, tests, evidence, acceptance, operations, maintenance, and retirement.\n- A functional-state model that distinguishes desired, defined, proposed, allocated, implemented, integrated, verified, validated, operational, degraded, blocked, superseded, and retired states.\n- A functional verification and validation basis that makes claims reproducible and distinguishes analysis or model evidence from implementation and observed-behavior evidence.\n- A durable handoff to Operational, Information, Data, Application, Software, Platform, Infrastructure, Integration, API, Event, Security, Privacy, Safety, Reliability, Resilience, Accessibility, Engineering, Testing, Operations, Maintenance, and Retirement work.",
-      "original_applicability": "- A system, service, product, organization, device, process, model, agent, or capability contains interacting behaviors, transformations, controls, decisions, or interfaces.\n- Requirements must be decomposed and allocated across people, functions, systems, components, services, devices, external providers, or lifecycle activities.\n- The system crosses software, hardware, data, human, organizational, domain, supplier, physical, or jurisdictional boundaries.\n- Functional behavior has material security, privacy, safety, accessibility, reliability, resilience, performance, support, maintenance, recovery, or retirement consequences.\n- Multiple implementations or architectural alternatives must satisfy the same outcome-level obligations.\n- AI, automation, orchestration, or external services may perform, recommend, constrain, or supervise a function.\n- Verification requires functional-level analysis, inspection, demonstration, simulation, testing, measurement, or traceability independent of implementation detail.",
