able.\n- Resource, capacity, risk, issue, decision, change, and stakeholder-management systems.\n- Durable inter-project handoffs and one authoritative state per program object.\n- An operational transition and benefits-realization plan extending beyond project delivery.","original_applicability":"- The desired outcome requires multiple interdependent projects, products, teams, vendors, agents, or organizational changes.\n- Shared architecture, data, platforms, environments, releases, customers, or operations create cross-project risk.\n- Benefits or mission outcomes appear only after integration and operational adoption.\n- Program-level capacity, sequencing, governance, or transition decisions are required.\n- A major migration, transformation, compliance effort, platform build, or system-of-systems initiative is underway.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-02-02","REQ-02-03","REQ-02-04","REQ-02-05","REQ-04-01","REQ-04-02","REQ-04-03","REQ-04-04","REQ-05-01","REQ-05-02","REQ-05-03","REQ-05-04","REQ-31-01","REQ-31-02","REQ-31-03","REQ-31-04","REQ-31-05","REQ-39-01","REQ-39-02","REQ-39-03","REQ-39-04","REQ-41-01","REQ-41-02","REQ-41-03","REQ-41-04"],"decision_reason":"Complete 277-line original reread after combined-output truncation; major platform/migration and cooperating workstreams require real integration/operational outcomes; actual current single executor, no invented reviewers, resources or benefit.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_04.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/strategy_and_delivery_20261006.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/009_PROJECT_MANAGEMENT.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/009_PROJECT_MANAGEMENT.txt","sha256":"21e7212a36fe904fcebde7dc91df47bd7f75ef887877e151ef6d3bd478c4fecf","bytes":17449,"section":"009","original_title":"SECTION 009 — Project Management","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":2,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_02_Research_Strategy_and_Risk.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A project charter defining objective, scope, exclusions, sponsor, project owner, authority, deliverables, and acceptance.\n- A current-state baseline and target state linked to requirements and evidence.\n- A dependency-aware work breakdown with bounded tasks, owners or roles, inputs, outputs, tools, permissions, and completion evidence.\n- A realistic sequence or schedule whose estimates disclose assumptions, confidence, capacity, review, rework, and external delay.\n- Integrated risk, issue, decision, change, quality, communication, and stakeholder controls.\n- Observable milestones representing achieved states rather than activity labels.\n- A verified delivery, operational handoff, closure, and continuity package.","original_applicability":"- A temporary, bounded body of work must create, change, migrate, validate, or retire a defined result.\n- Work requires sequencing, resource coordination, dependencies, milestones, risk, and acceptance.\n- Multiple human or AI roles contribute to one deliverable or outcome.\n- A change has material cost, schedule, operational, security, safety, legal, or stakeholder consequences.\n- A project requires an explicit start, controlled execution, and closure or transfer.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-00-01","REQ-00-02","REQ-00-03","REQ-00-04","REQ-00-05","REQ-02-01","REQ-02-02","REQ-02-03","REQ-02-04","REQ-02-05","REQ-09-01","REQ-09-02","REQ-09-03","REQ-09-04","REQ-18-01","REQ-18-02","REQ-18-03","REQ-18-04","REQ-39-01","REQ-39-02","REQ-39-03","REQ-39-04","REQ-40-01","REQ-40-02","REQ-40-03","REQ-41-01","REQ-41-02","REQ-41-03","REQ-41-04","REQ-42-01","REQ-42-02","REQ-42-03"],"decision_reason":"Temporary bounded ground-up CETA work has owner, dependencies, preservation, staged delivery and verified handoff obligations; existing project ledgers apply. No schedule, capacity, reviewer or acceptance invented.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_05.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/domain_feasibility_and_risk_20261006.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/010_PRODUCT_MANAGEMENT.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/010_PRODUCT_MANAGEMENT.txt","sha256":"508e2d45ef9482e1c3ea84a8e2192b943829a31ea653922f71d98b8214fa8e33","bytes":17199,"section":"010","original_title":"SECTION 010 — Product Management","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":2,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_02_Research_Strategy_and_Risk.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A current product decision system with explicit owner, users, outcomes, principles, capabilities, constraints, and lifecycle state.\n- A prioritized and traceable opportunity, problem, capability, and requirement flow rather than an unbounded request queue.\n- A roadmap and release logic tied to dependencies, evidence, risk, readiness, and learning.\n- Clear definitions of discovery, experiment, planned, committed, implemented, tested, verified, released, degraded, deprecated, and retired states.\n- Closed feedback loops across research, usage, support, incidents, operations, market, and stakeholder evidence.\n- Bounded human-AI product behavior, authority, correction, transparency, fallback, and recovery where applicable.\n- Lifecycle decisions and user obligations preserved through change and retirement.","original_applicability":"- A product is being discovered, built, operated, changed, supported, monetized, deprecated, or retired.\n- Requests, problems, opportunities, defects, incidents, risks, or strategic bets compete for prioritization.\n- Product decisions require coordination across research, design, engineering, security, operations, and business.\n- AI or automation materially affects user experience, decisions, data, authority, or trust.\n- Product commitments must remain current despite changing evidence, providers, markets, or constraints.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-00-01","REQ-00-02","REQ-00-03","REQ-00-04","REQ-00-05","REQ-03-01","REQ-03-02","REQ-03-03","REQ-03-04","REQ-04-01","REQ-04-02","REQ-04-03","REQ-04-04","REQ-23-01","REQ-23-02","REQ-23-03","REQ-23-04","REQ-24-01","REQ-24-02","REQ-24-03","REQ-24-04","REQ-24-05","REQ-29-01","REQ-29-02","REQ-29-03","REQ-30-01","REQ-30-02","REQ-30-03","REQ-30-04","REQ-31-01","REQ-31-02","REQ-31-03","REQ-31-04","REQ-31-05","REQ-32-01","REQ-32-02","REQ-32-03","REQ-32-04","REQ-37-01","REQ-37-02","REQ-37-03","REQ-37-04","REQ-38-01","REQ-38-02","REQ-38-03","REQ-38-04","REQ-38-05","REQ-41-01","REQ-41-02","REQ-41-03","REQ-41-04"],"decision_reason":"The complete native product changes continuing user journeys, data, model capability, safety, support and lifecycle; source test activity does not establish product outcomes or release readiness.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_05.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/domain_feasibility_and_risk_20261006.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/011_BUSINESS_ANALYSIS.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/011_BUSINESS_ANALYSIS.txt","sha256":"b1188258307528e879b7c50e7a85cbd1345db5599f2fe1f4b64d20ab76df1475","bytes":16959,"section":"011","original_title":"SECTION 011 — Business Analysis","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":2,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_02_Research_Strategy_and_Risk.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A bounded analysis question, scope, decision owner, affected parties, and current-state baseline.\n- A stakeholder, actor, capability, process, information, decision, rule, and obligation model.\n- A target-state definition expressed as outcomes and capabilities rather than an assumed solution.\n- A gap and impact analysis covering people, process, information, technology, policy, risk, and operations.\n- Options evaluated consistently with assumptions, dependencies, constraints, feasibility, risk, and reversibility.\n- Traceable business needs and rules ready for domain analysis and requirements engineering.\n- A decision and continuity package preserving unresolved questions, dissent, and evidence.","original_applicability":"- A strategic, product, program, project, process, policy, or system change must be understood before design.\n- Stakeholder statements conflict or use ambiguous business and domain terminology.\n- Current workflows, rules, decisions, data, roles, or pain points are not adequately documented.\n- A technology or AI proposal may alter work, authority, accountability, risk, or user outcomes.\n- Requirements must be derived from real organizational outcomes and constraints.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-00-01","REQ-00-02","REQ-00-03","REQ-00-04","REQ-00-05","REQ-01-01","REQ-01-02","REQ-01-03","REQ-01-04","REQ-01-05","REQ-04-01","REQ-04-02","REQ-04-03","REQ-04-04","REQ-09-01","REQ-09-02","REQ-09-03","REQ-09-04","REQ-10-01","REQ-10-02","REQ-10-03","REQ-10-04","REQ-11-01","REQ-11-02","REQ-11-03","REQ-11-04","REQ-11-05","REQ-31-01","REQ-31-02","REQ-31-03","REQ-31-04","REQ-31-05","REQ-39-01","REQ-39-02","REQ-39-03","REQ-39-04","REQ-41-01","REQ-41-02","REQ-41-03","REQ-41-04"],"decision_reason":"Ground-up authority and workflow change requires stated/observed/inferred/accepted needs and current/target state distinctions; existing implementation is evidence, not automatically accepted domain truth.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_05.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/domain_feasibility_and_risk_20261006.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/012_MARKET_RESEARCH.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/012_MARKET_RESEARCH.txt","sha256":"d36355ed02ce22c1f1327aafa01e1ea7aa20f74256ac9a93dfe8c49e745773e1","bytes":16928,"section":"012","original_title":"SECTION 012 — Market Research","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"NOT_APPLICABLE","implementation_status":"NOT_STARTED","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":2,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_02_Research_Strategy_and_Risk.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A decision-linked market research question, scope, target population, geography, jurisdiction, and timeframe.\n- A research design with source hierarchy, sampling, inclusion, exclusion, collection, analysis, privacy, consent, and quality controls.\n- A current market and alternative baseline with definitions that prevent category inflation.\n- Segment, need, behavior, adoption, channel, price or funding, and barrier findings bounded by evidence.\n- Conflicting evidence, sampling limitations, bias, uncertainty, and time sensitivity made visible.\n- A conclusion and recommendation whose confidence does not exceed the method.\n- A reproducible evidence and continuity package for later strategy and product decisions.","original_applicability":"- A business, product, portfolio, investment, market-entry, pricing, channel, or positioning decision depends on external evidence.\n- The size, characteristics, needs, behavior, willingness, adoption, or constraints of a population are uncertain.\n- Market conditions, regulations, competitors, substitutes, or technologies may have changed.\n- A product may serve new geographies, cultures, languages, accessibility contexts, or user groups.\n- Claims of demand, growth, pricing, market share, or adoption will influence material commitments.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":[],"decision_reason":"Scoped to implementation for the known current owner; no market-entry, pricing, funding, population-demand, market-share or adoption claim selected. Original non-applicability assigns single-known-user workflow to user research. Reopen for market, population, channel, demand or material distribution decisions using market evidence; no permanent waiver.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_05.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/domain_feasibility_and_risk_20261006.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/013_COMPETITIVE_INTELLIGENCE.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/013_COMPETITIVE_INTELLIGENCE.txt","sha256":"fadf23d5d8647be0b7dfd742d3b1a0c548c8333d13330cae80b9c94cee6492d8","bytes":16747,"section":"013","original_title":"SECTION 013 — Competitive Intelligence","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"NOT_APPLICABLE","implementation_status":"NOT_STARTED","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":2,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_02_Research_Strategy_and_Risk.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A bounded intelligence question, scope, named subjects, timeframe, jurisdiction, authority, and collection rules.\n- A source-grounded profile of competitors, substitutes, alternatives, ecosystems, and relevant capabilities.\n- A comparison framework using consistent criteria and explicit evidence states.\n- Clear separation among claimed, documented, demonstrated, independently verified, inferred, and unknown capability.\n- Analysis of positioning, business model, technology, product, operations, partnerships, risks, likely responses, and strategic implications where relevant.\n- Legal, ethical, security, privacy, licensing, and source-handling compliance.\n- A recommendation and monitoring plan that does not turn comparison into imitation.","original_applicability":"- Strategy, positioning, product, partnership, procurement, investment, pricing, or risk decisions depend on external actors or alternatives.\n- A competitor or substitute may materially change user expectations, market structure, technology, regulation, supply, or security.\n- Claims of differentiation, parity, advantage, or threat require evidence.\n- The ecosystem includes providers, platforms, models, standards, distributors, integrators, open-source projects, or adjacent entrants.\n- Continuous monitoring is required for defined high-impact signals.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":[],"decision_reason":"Current mission/user/feasibility direction does not depend on a selected competitor intelligence question, parity, differentiation or market-position claim. Original non-applicability fits this bounded decision. Dependency/security/license qualification remains required. Reopen for competitive claim, procurement/ecosystem decision relying on alternatives or authorized monitoring.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_05.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/domain_feasibility_and_risk_20261006.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/014_STAKEHOLDER_MANAGEMENT.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/014_STAKEHOLDER_MANAGEMENT.txt","sha256":"52b9bdffd0de0a4d68a2e109b5734621d52f8c7e97a2a5de51ebb9243f31ef4b","bytes":18293,"section":"014","original_title":"SECTION 014 — Stakeholder Management","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":2,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_02_Research_Strategy_and_Risk.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A stakeholder register with stable identities or protected classifications, roles, authority, interests, impacts, obligations, communication needs, and current relationship state.\n- A distinction among decision owner, sponsor, approver, operator, user, subject, beneficiary, affected non-user, regulator, partner, vendor, reviewer, and informed party.\n- An engagement and communication plan proportional to impact, accessibility, language, culture, urgency, and consequence.\n- Consent, consultation, notification, grievance, appeal, conflict, and escalation requirements where applicable.\n- A commitment register linking promises, owners, evidence, due conditions, changes, and closure.\n- Material disagreement, power imbalance, exclusion, and future affected-party considerations made visible.\n- Durable handoff and relationship continuity across human and AI interactions.","original_applicability":"- The work affects or depends on more than the direct requester.\n- Approval, consent, consultation, notification, coordination, adoption, support, regulation, or public trust matters.\n- Power, risk, benefit, burden, access, language, culture, accessibility, or information is unevenly distributed.\n- Multiple human or AI actors communicate, negotiate, recommend, or act across boundaries.\n- Commitments or long-lived relationships must survive staff, agent, vendor, or lifecycle changes.\n- Future users, operators, communities, or intelligent systems may inherit material consequences and should be represented where legitimate and possible.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-00-01","REQ-00-02","REQ-00-03","REQ-00-04","REQ-00-05","REQ-06-01","REQ-06-02","REQ-06-03","REQ-06-04","REQ-09-01","REQ-09-02","REQ-09-03","REQ-09-04","REQ-13-01","REQ-13-02","REQ-13-03","REQ-13-04","REQ-13-05","REQ-17-01","REQ-17-02","REQ-17-03","REQ-29-01","REQ-29-02","REQ-29-03","REQ-32-01","REQ-32-02","REQ-32-03","REQ-32-04","REQ-33-01","REQ-33-02","REQ-33-03","REQ-39-01","REQ-39-02","REQ-39-03","REQ-39-04","REQ-42-01","REQ-42-02","REQ-42-03"],"decision_reason":"Known owner plus future affected users/operators/maintainers and independent/domain reviewers require accountable commitments, dissent, privacy, correction and scoped authority. Only current owner and executor are established; no contacts, silence-as-consent, reviewer independence or representation invented.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_05.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/domain_feasibility_and_risk_20261006.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/015_USER_RESEARCH.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/015_USER_RESEARCH.txt","sha256":"d0868e2e9561d4fd27554856d8f182edb89bc55aa608dd44672742cc9ae1bb84","bytes":27902,"section":"015","original_title":"SECTION 015 — User Research","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":2,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_02_Research_Strategy_and_Risk.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A research brief linking the user-research question to a specific product, service, system, policy, or design decision.\n- An explicit population and participant plan covering intended users, owner and operator roles, affected non-users, excluded groups, accessibility, language, culture, geography, and context.\n- A lawful and ethical method with informed consent, safe withdrawal, privacy, security, compensation, retention, and AI-use disclosure where applicable.\n- Evidence-grounded user jobs, goals, journeys, mental models, terminology, decision points, pain, workarounds, risks, and recovery expectations.\n- Observed normal, boundary, failure, denial, interruption, and recovery behavior rather than happy-path preference alone.\n- Findings separated from researcher interpretation, product recommendation, assumptions, and unresolved questions.\n- A traceable set of design and product implications ready for Business Analysis, Domain Analysis, Requirements Engineering, UX, accessibility, safety, and validation work.\n- A durable research repository and continuity record that protects participants while allowing later reviewers to understand the basis and limits of each finding.","original_applicability":"- A product, service, workflow, interface, AI system, policy, or operational process is intended for or materially affects people.\n- User needs, jobs, language, mental models, trust, accessibility, or failure behavior are uncertain.\n- A design or automation proposal may change human decisions, workload, authority, accountability, privacy, safety, or recovery.\n- Existing analytics, support data, stakeholder statements, or internal assumptions do not explain why users behave as observed.\n- The system serves multiple roles, contexts, cultures, languages, devices, abilities, or levels of expertise.\n- A release, redesign, migration, deprecation, or retirement may disrupt complete user or owner journeys.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-03-01","REQ-03-02","REQ-03-03","REQ-03-04","REQ-09-01","REQ-09-02","REQ-09-03","REQ-09-04","REQ-11-01","REQ-11-02","REQ-11-03","REQ-11-04","REQ-11-05","REQ-24-01","REQ-24-02","REQ-24-03","REQ-24-04","REQ-24-05","REQ-29-01","REQ-29-02","REQ-29-03","REQ-30-01","REQ-30-02","REQ-30-03","REQ-30-04","REQ-32-01","REQ-32-02","REQ-32-03","REQ-32-04","REQ-33-01","REQ-33-02","REQ-33-03","REQ-36-01","REQ-36-02","REQ-36-03","REQ-36-04","REQ-41-01","REQ-41-02","REQ-41-03","REQ-41-04"],"decision_reason":"Human-facing native operation requires actual comprehension, task, denial, stop, recovery and accessibility evidence. Direct instructions are stated needs, not observed usability or participant research. No interviews, personas, representative users, consent or private research data invented.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_05.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/domain_feasibility_and_risk_20261006.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/016_DOMAIN_ANALYSIS.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/016_DOMAIN_ANALYSIS.txt","sha256":"14a2c01beb7770400c7fd3fd7a6487f0e5b084b91f7e2485d3e889019a53dd72","bytes":31016,"section":"016","original_title":"SECTION 016 — Domain Analysis","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":2,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_02_Research_Strategy_and_Risk.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A bounded domain statement identifying purpose, scope, excluded domains, context, decision owner, authoritative experts, and intended downstream use.\n- A canonical terminology and definition set with synonyms, overloaded terms, prohibited ambiguity, source, owner, version, and jurisdiction or context.\n- An actor, responsibility, accountability, capability, permission, authority, and affected-party model that distinguishes human, organizational, system, agent, and external actors.\n- A concept model covering entities, value objects, classifications, relationships, identifiers, ownership, and lifecycle.\n- A behavior model covering commands or intents, events, states, transitions, decisions, business or domain rules, invariants, exceptions, escalation, recovery, and termination.\n- A domain-boundary and context map identifying internal subdomains, bounded contexts, integrations, external authorities, shared language, translations, and anti-corruption needs.\n- An information model covering source of truth, provenance, quality, timing, retention, privacy, security, jurisdiction, and evidence meaning.\n- Validated examples, counterexamples, edge cases, disputed interpretations, unresolved questions, and change triggers.\n- A handoff package that allows Requirements Engineering and Domain Architecture to proceed without inventing meaning or authority.","original_applicability":"- The system operates in a field with specialized terminology, rules, states, decisions, exceptions, obligations, or external authorities.\n- Stakeholders use conflicting or overloaded terms, or current systems encode inconsistent meanings.\n- Requirements, architecture, data models, APIs, events, workflows, policy, AI behavior, or tests require a shared domain model.\n- The work crosses organizational, jurisdictional, product, service, platform, physical, scientific, legal, financial, health, safety, or operational boundaries.\n- A migration, integration, modernization, automation, or AI system may preserve or alter domain behavior.\n- Errors in meaning, authority, state, evidence, or exception handling could create material harm.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-04-01","REQ-04-02","REQ-04-03","REQ-04-04","REQ-05-01","REQ-05-02","REQ-05-03","REQ-05-04","REQ-06-01","REQ-06-02","REQ-06-03","REQ-06-04","REQ-07-01","REQ-07-02","REQ-07-03","REQ-07-04","REQ-07-05","REQ-09-01","REQ-09-02","REQ-09-03","REQ-09-04","REQ-10-01","REQ-10-02","REQ-10-03","REQ-10-04","REQ-11-01","REQ-11-02","REQ-11-03","REQ-11-04","REQ-11-05","REQ-12-01","REQ-12-02","REQ-12-03","REQ-12-04","REQ-12-05","REQ-16-01","REQ-16-02","REQ-16-03","REQ-16-04","REQ-18-01","REQ-18-02","REQ-18-03","REQ-18-04","REQ-19-01","REQ-19-02","REQ-19-03","REQ-19-04","REQ-19-05","REQ-20-01","REQ-20-02","REQ-20-03","REQ-20-04","REQ-25-01","REQ-25-02","REQ-25-03","REQ-25-04","REQ-26-01","REQ-26-02","REQ-26-03","REQ-26-04","REQ-27-01","REQ-27-02","REQ-27-03","REQ-27-04","REQ-30-01","REQ-30-02","REQ-30-03","REQ-30-04","REQ-31-01","REQ-31-02","REQ-31-03","REQ-31-04","REQ-31-05","REQ-35-01","REQ-35-02","REQ-35-03"],"decision_reason":"Actor, role, authority, capability, scope, state, effect, evidence and lifecycle meanings must agree across contracts, kernel, store, transport and UI. Existing schemas represent the domain but do not authenticate or enforce it. Complete bounded reread replaced the truncated display; semantic validation and independent/domain review remain open.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_05.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/domain_feasibility_and_risk_20261006.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/017_FEASIBILITY_ANALYSIS.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/017_FEASIBILITY_ANALYSIS.txt","sha256":"7943479bd9845da9853bd1b68d2aa6f009f04caa0d756b118ac54e0dcd7ec409","bytes":33925,"section":"017","original_title":"SECTION 017 — Feasibility Analysis","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":2,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_02_Research_Strategy_and_Risk.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A bounded feasibility question identifying the objective, decision owner, scope, options, exclusions, environment, horizon, and required confidence.\n- A current-state baseline distinguishing confirmed capability, available capacity, existing obligations, missing prerequisites, unresolved assumptions, and inaccessible evidence.\n- A multidimensional feasibility assessment covering technical, architectural, data, AI or model, operational, human, accessibility, security, privacy, safety, legal, regulatory, economic, schedule, resource, supply-chain, integration, migration, support, maintenance, and retirement concerns as applicable.\n- An option-by-option constraint map identifying mandatory conditions, disqualifying conditions, dependencies, tradeoffs, reversibility, points of no return, and evidence strength.\n- A cheapest-safe resolution plan for material unknowns using inspection, prototype, simulation, proof of concept, benchmark, pilot, test, consultation, or staged experiment.\n- A truthful determination such as FEASIBLE, FEASIBLE WITH CONDITIONS, LIMITED PILOT ONLY, ADDITIONAL EVIDENCE REQUIRED, NOT FEASIBLE WITHIN DECLARED CONSTRAINTS, or OUT OF SCOPE.\n- A handoff package stating which conditions must become requirements, architecture obligations, risk treatments, acceptance criteria, owner decisions, or stop conditions.","original_applicability":"- A proposed objective requires material investment, irreversible commitment, external dependency, regulated activity, consequential automation, or long-lived operation.\n- Multiple options exist and their viability depends on different constraints, evidence, resources, or risks.\n- A new product, platform, architecture, model, device, integration, migration, deployment, manufacturing path, or retirement plan is being considered.\n- The work depends on uncertain technology, model behavior, data, performance, scale, staffing, budget, schedule, supply chain, jurisdiction, or user adoption.\n- A prototype or demonstration must be translated into a production, offline, field, safety-critical, regulated, or owner-operable system.\n- Failure to discover infeasibility early would create material cost, harm, lock-in, delay, data loss, broken commitments, or loss of trust.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-03-01","REQ-03-02","REQ-03-03","REQ-03-04","REQ-04-01","REQ-04-02","REQ-04-03","REQ-04-04","REQ-21-01","REQ-21-02","REQ-21-03","REQ-21-04","REQ-21-05","REQ-22-01","REQ-22-02","REQ-22-03","REQ-22-04","REQ-23-01","REQ-23-02","REQ-23-03","REQ-23-04","REQ-24-01","REQ-24-02","REQ-24-03","REQ-24-04","REQ-24-05","REQ-25-01","REQ-25-02","REQ-25-03","REQ-25-04","REQ-26-01","REQ-26-02","REQ-26-03","REQ-26-04","REQ-27-01","REQ-27-02","REQ-27-03","REQ-27-04","REQ-34-01","REQ-34-02","REQ-34-03","REQ-34-04","REQ-36-01","REQ-36-02","REQ-36-03","REQ-36-04","REQ-37-01","REQ-37-02","REQ-37-03","REQ-37-04","REQ-38-01","REQ-38-02","REQ-38-03","REQ-38-04","REQ-38-05","REQ-41-01","REQ-41-02","REQ-41-03","REQ-41-04","REQ-42-01","REQ-42-02","REQ-42-03"],"decision_reason":"Whole native/offline persistent system depends on unqualified actual model, storage, OS boundaries, IPC, custody, distribution, resources and endurance. Current determination ADDITIONAL_EVIDENCE_REQUIRED; pure tests establish only bounded construction evidence, no whole-system feasibility, impossibility or budget/timeline invented.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_05.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/domain_feasibility_and_risk_20261006.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/018_RISK_MANAGEMENT.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/018_RISK_MANAGEMENT.txt","sha256":"ff8feebbf815f4bb8edccbf5890c0ab756fb3c884a52c7abed80be2ecede999f","bytes":34643,"section":"018","original_title":"SECTION 018 — Risk Management","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":2,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_02_Research_Strategy_and_Risk.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A bounded risk-management context identifying objective, scope, assets and values at risk, affected parties, authority, risk owners, time horizon, environments, lifecycle stages, and governing constraints.\n- A common taxonomy distinguishing risk, issue, hazard, threat, vulnerability, exposure, defect, control failure, assumption, unknown, dependency, blocker, opportunity, and residual risk.\n- A traceable risk register containing source, cause, event, consequence, affected objective or party, likelihood basis, impact basis, uncertainty, velocity, detectability, interdependencies, owner, treatment, trigger, threshold, evidence, status, and review date.\n- Explicit treatment decisions: avoid, reduce, transfer or share where legitimate, accept, exploit or enable an opportunity, defer with conditions, escalate, or prohibit.\n- Control and contingency plans covering prevention, protection, detection, response, recovery, communication, rollback, restore, fallback, and safe halt.\n- Risk aggregation and dependency analysis addressing correlated, cascading, systemic, concentration, common-mode, supply-chain, model, data, human, and lifecycle risks.\n- A truthful residual-risk statement and an attributable human acceptance, rejection, or escalation decision where acceptance authority exists.\n- A handoff package that turns risk treatment into requirements, architecture controls, tests, observability, operational procedures, maintenance obligations, and retirement conditions.","original_applicability":"- An objective, decision, system, change, experiment, deployment, migration, operation, maintenance action, or retirement path has uncertain outcomes with material consequences.\n- Work involves security, privacy, safety, accessibility, legal, regulatory, financial, environmental, reputational, operational, data, AI/model, supply-chain, or human-impact exposure.\n- Multiple dependencies, providers, components, teams, agents, environments, or lifecycle stages can create correlated or cascading failure.\n- A decision requires explicit residual-risk acceptance, conditional approval, escalation, or stop criteria.\n- Existing controls, assumptions, evidence, or operating conditions may drift over time.\n- Failure could affect people who do not directly use or control the system.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-19-01","REQ-19-02","REQ-19-03","REQ-19-04","REQ-19-05","REQ-20-01","REQ-20-02","REQ-20-03","REQ-20-04","REQ-21-01","REQ-21-02","REQ-21-03","REQ-21-04","REQ-21-05","REQ-23-01","REQ-23-02","REQ-23-03","REQ-23-04","REQ-25-01","REQ-25-02","REQ-25-03","REQ-25-04","REQ-26-01","REQ-26-02","REQ-26-03","REQ-26-04","REQ-27-01","REQ-27-02","REQ-27-03","REQ-27-04","REQ-28-01","REQ-28-02","REQ-28-03","REQ-29-01","REQ-29-02","REQ-29-03","REQ-30-01","REQ-30-02","REQ-30-03","REQ-30-04","REQ-31-01","REQ-31-02","REQ-31-03","REQ-31-04","REQ-31-05","REQ-34-01","REQ-34-02","REQ-34-03","REQ-34-04","REQ-35-01","REQ-35-02","REQ-35-03","REQ-36-01","REQ-36-02","REQ-36-03","REQ-36-04","REQ-37-01","REQ-37-02","REQ-37-03","REQ-37-04","REQ-38-01","REQ-38-02","REQ-38-03","REQ-38-04","REQ-38-05","REQ-41-01","REQ-41-02","REQ-41-03","REQ-41-04","REQ-42-01","REQ-42-02","REQ-42-03"],"decision_reason":"Consequential authority/data/model/OS/migration paths require causal risks, control effectiveness, correlated failure, recovery and scoped acceptance distinct from confirmed issues and unknowns. No numerical rating, risk acceptance or operating approval inferred from continued implementation.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_05.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/domain_feasibility_and_risk_20261006.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/019_REQUIREMENTS_ENGINEERING.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/019_REQUIREMENTS_ENGINEERING.txt","sha256":"20a68a5720dba445cb319405ae738fbfdb7dbbad8bed2916713690bf54ba0755","bytes":47130,"section":"019","original_title":"SECTION 019 — Requirements Engineering","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":3,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_03_Requirements_through_Hardware_Architecture.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A requirements context identifying the authorized objective, decision owners, affected parties, system boundary, environments, lifecycle scope, applicable authorities, constraints, and intended acceptance state.\n- A controlled hierarchy connecting mission, business, stakeholder, user, domain, system, subsystem, component, interface, data, AI, governance, security, privacy, safety, accessibility, operational, support, maintenance, migration, deprecation, and retirement requirements as applicable.\n- Atomic, unambiguous, necessary, feasible, consistent, verifiable, attributable, versioned requirements with stable identifiers, source, rationale, applicability, criticality, owner, acceptance criteria, verification method, evidence expectations, dependencies, and status.\n- Explicit separation among needs, goals, requirements, constraints, assumptions, design decisions, implementation tasks, tests, preferences, recommendations, and unresolved questions.\n- Bidirectional traceability from authoritative source and affected-party evidence through requirements, architecture, implementation, tests, evidence, acceptance, operation, change, and retirement.\n- A conflict, dissent, ambiguity, gap, waiver, exception, and non-applicability record that prevents unresolved conditions from disappearing through synthesis.\n- A governed baseline and change process that identifies impact, invalidates stale approvals and evidence, preserves history, and routes material changes to legitimate authority.\n- A complete handoff to Systems Engineering and later architecture, design, implementation, testing, verification, operations, maintenance, and retirement work without forcing downstream roles to invent missing objectives or boundaries.","original_applicability":"- A product, service, system, platform, application, device, process, model, agent, integration, migration, deployment, operational capability, maintenance change, or retirement action must be defined before design or implementation.\n- Multiple people, systems, environments, jurisdictions, data classes, interfaces, or lifecycle stages create different obligations or variants.\n- Stakeholder statements, user findings, domain rules, feasibility conditions, or risk treatments must become actionable and verifiable.\n- Work has material security, privacy, safety, accessibility, governance, legal, financial, environmental, reliability, recovery, or human-impact consequences.\n- Existing requirements are incomplete, contradictory, stale, untraceable, implementation-specific, or disconnected from tests and evidence.\n- A change may invalidate architecture, code, data, models, tests, approvals, procedures, training, support, or retirement plans.\n- AI systems participate in analysis, recommendation, communication, content generation, tool use, autonomous action, or decisions affecting people or protected state.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-01-01","REQ-01-02","REQ-01-03","REQ-01-04","REQ-01-05","REQ-04-01","REQ-04-02","REQ-04-03","REQ-04-04","REQ-39-01","REQ-39-04"],"decision_reason":"Whole product rebuild requires governed atomic requirements, traceability and change control; full 490-line original inspected; upstream 001-018 and baseline acceptance remain open.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_02.md"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/020_SYSTEMS_ENGINEERING.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/020_SYSTEMS_ENGINEERING.txt","sha256":"a9ebeaa75fab0bd1a6d7c6b8e8e3642491f6f51523a65f22cda9eb297f82bbe7","bytes":58875,"section":"020","original_title":"SECTION 020 — Systems Engineering","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":3,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_03_Requirements_through_Hardware_Architecture.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A declared system of interest with purpose, owner, boundary, environments, users, operators, affected parties, external systems, enabling systems, lifecycle stages, and non-goals.\n- An operational concept describing how the system will be used, supervised, supported, degraded, recovered, changed, and retired through complete mission and owner journeys.\n- A system context model showing external actors, systems, organizations, data sources, authorities, dependencies, interfaces, trust boundaries, failure domains, and sources of truth.\n- A controlled allocation of approved requirements, constraints, risk treatments, feasibility conditions, and acceptance obligations to system functions, elements, interfaces, people, processes, and lifecycle activities.\n- Functional, logical, physical, operational, information, data, authority, control, evidence, and lifecycle views that are consistent with one another and traceable to the requirements baseline.\n- Defined system elements with responsibilities, inputs, outputs, dependencies, resources, interfaces, states, failure behavior, recovery behavior, configuration identity, ownership, and verification method.\n- A versioned interface control model covering technical, human, organizational, data, event, model, authority, evidence, security, safety, privacy, accessibility, and operational interfaces as applicable.\n- A lifecycle model integrating concept, development, acquisition, implementation, integration, verification, validation, transition, operation, support, maintenance, modernization, migration, deprecation, decommissioning, disposal, and retirement.\n- A technical management system for baselines, configuration, decisions, risks, opportunities, changes, reviews, measures, evidence, deviations, waivers, nonconformities, and continuity.\n- A system integration, verification, validation, transition, operations, support, recovery, and retirement strategy sufficient to guide downstream architecture and engineering without forcing those disciplines to invent missing system obligations.\n- Explicit separation among system need, requirement, architecture, design, implementation, integration, verification, validation, acceptance, operation, and observed outcome.\n- A bounded human-AI operating model in which AI capability, assigned role, delegated authority, execution permission, evidence, and final human decision authority remain distinct.","original_applicability":"- A product, platform, application, service, device, model, agent, robot, embedded system, infrastructure, organizational process, or socio-technical capability contains multiple interacting elements or lifecycle stages.\n- The system crosses software, hardware, data, AI, human, organizational, physical, operational, supplier, or jurisdictional boundaries.\n- Multiple teams, roles, vendors, models, repositories, environments, or authorities must produce one integrated outcome.\n- Requirements must be allocated among system elements and verified at different levels.\n- Interfaces, dependencies, trust boundaries, failure domains, external services, or sources of truth materially affect success.\n- Security, privacy, safety, accessibility, governance, compliance, reliability, resilience, supportability, maintainability, recoverability, or environmental obligations span more than one component.\n- The system must operate under degraded, disconnected, emergency, maintenance, migration, or retirement conditions.\n- A change to one element may invalidate system behavior, interfaces, evidence, approvals, training, operations, or lifecycle plans.\n- AI or autonomous functions can recommend, decide, communicate, control tools, modify state, affect people, or influence protected outcomes.\n- Acquisition, procurement, manufacturing, deployment, transition, support, modernization, decommissioning, or disposal requires coordinated technical evidence.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-00-01","REQ-00-02","REQ-00-03","REQ-00-04","REQ-00-05","REQ-01-01","REQ-01-02","REQ-01-03","REQ-01-04","REQ-01-05","REQ-02-01","REQ-02-02","REQ-02-03","REQ-02-04","REQ-02-05","REQ-03-01","REQ-03-02","REQ-03-03","REQ-03-04","REQ-04-01","REQ-04-02","REQ-04-03","REQ-04-04","REQ-05-01","REQ-05-02","REQ-05-03","REQ-05-04","REQ-06-01","REQ-06-02","REQ-06-03","REQ-06-04","REQ-07-01","REQ-07-02","REQ-07-03","REQ-07-04","REQ-07-05","REQ-08-01","REQ-08-02","REQ-08-03","REQ-08-04","REQ-09-01","REQ-09-02","REQ-09-03","REQ-09-04","REQ-10-01","REQ-10-02","REQ-10-03","REQ-10-04","REQ-11-01","REQ-11-02","REQ-11-03","REQ-11-04","REQ-11-05","REQ-12-01","REQ-12-02","REQ-12-03","REQ-12-04","REQ-12-05","REQ-13-01","REQ-13-02","REQ-13-03","REQ-13-04","REQ-13-05","REQ-14-01","REQ-14-02","REQ-14-03","REQ-15-01","REQ-15-02","REQ-15-03","REQ-16-01","REQ-16-02","REQ-16-03","REQ-16-04"