","original_title":"SECTION 021 — Enterprise Architecture","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"SUPPORTING","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":3,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_03_Requirements_through_Hardware_Architecture.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A declared enterprise boundary identifying mission, owners, operating environments, business units, products, programs, platforms, partners, suppliers, regulators, users, operators, affected parties, external systems, and lifecycle horizon.\n- An enterprise operating model showing how value is created, decisions are made, responsibilities are distributed, capabilities are owned, information flows, shared services are governed, and exceptions are handled.\n- A capability map with stable names, definitions, owners, outcomes, maturity or condition, dependencies, criticality, evidence, and lifecycle state.\n- Value-stream and journey views connecting mission and stakeholder outcomes to capabilities, processes, information, applications, technology, people, controls, evidence, and operational results.\n- Current-state, target-state, and transition-state architectures that distinguish observed fact, verified condition, inference, assumption, recommendation, approved decision, and unresolved unknown.\n- Explicit architecture principles, constraints, standards, reference patterns, decision rights, exception paths, and review triggers.\n- Portfolio-level alignment among strategies, investments, programs, products, platforms, data, security, governance, operations, maintenance, modernization, migration, deprecation, and retirement.\n- A traceable architecture roadmap with transition increments, dependencies, prerequisites, evidence gates, owner decisions, rollback or recovery conditions, and retirement obligations.\n- A durable architecture repository containing decisions, models, standards, waivers, exceptions, evidence, dissent, risks, dependencies, and supersession history.\n- A bounded human-AI operating model in which AI can analyze and propose enterprise structures without converting capability into authority, preferences into policy, or generated models into verified enterprise truth.","original_applicability":"- Multiple products, programs, business units, platforms, applications, data domains, suppliers, or operating environments must support one mission or portfolio.\n- Strategic objectives require coordinated changes across people, process, information, technology, governance, finance, security, operations, or lifecycle management.\n- Shared capabilities, platforms, standards, services, data, identity, evidence, or infrastructure are being created, consolidated, separated, migrated, or retired.\n- Existing architecture is fragmented, undocumented, inconsistent, duplicative, vendor-dependent, difficult to operate, or disconnected from strategy and owner outcomes.\n- Investments or modernization decisions require comparison across current, target, and transition states.\n- Regulatory, legal, privacy, safety, security, accessibility, resilience, or records obligations apply across more than one system or organizational boundary.\n- AI systems, agents, models, or automation influence enterprise decisions, shared data, common services, workforce processes, customer outcomes, or protected state.\n- A merger, acquisition, divestiture, partnership, outsourcing arrangement, major reorganization, or platform transition changes enterprise boundaries or dependencies.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-03-01","REQ-03-02","REQ-03-03","REQ-03-04","REQ-04-01","REQ-04-02","REQ-04-03","REQ-04-04","REQ-05-01","REQ-05-02","REQ-05-03","REQ-05-04","REQ-12-01","REQ-12-02","REQ-12-03","REQ-12-04","REQ-12-05","REQ-19-01","REQ-19-02","REQ-19-03","REQ-19-04","REQ-19-05","REQ-23-01","REQ-23-02","REQ-23-03","REQ-23-04","REQ-31-01","REQ-31-02","REQ-31-03","REQ-31-04","REQ-31-05","REQ-38-01","REQ-38-02","REQ-38-03","REQ-38-04","REQ-38-05","REQ-39-01","REQ-39-02","REQ-39-03","REQ-39-04","REQ-41-01","REQ-41-02","REQ-41-03","REQ-41-04"],"decision_reason":"Complete 301-line original read. The coordinated people, process, information, technology, governance and lifecycle concerns support CETA architecture. Small size is not an exemption. Current authority does not include organization-wide policy, investments, portfolio consolidation or other personal repositories; no approved enterprise baseline is assumed.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_07.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/architecture_contract_reconciliation_20261006_07.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/022_SOLUTION_ARCHITECTURE.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/022_SOLUTION_ARCHITECTURE.txt","sha256":"de0b92e05a788722df6bdbffefacfeb7e45c9f06d26ff3a03c98a7bf68e2b2ed","bytes":26329,"section":"022","original_title":"SECTION 022 — Solution Architecture","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":3,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_03_Requirements_through_Hardware_Architecture.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A bounded solution context identifying objective, owner, users, operators, affected parties, environments, external systems, enterprise services, constraints, non-goals, and lifecycle horizon.\n- A requirements-to-architecture trace showing how every material requirement, risk treatment, feasibility condition, and acceptance obligation maps to one or more solution elements or remains visibly unresolved.\n- Credible solution alternatives evaluated using consistent criteria, evidence, assumptions, cost, complexity, risk, human impact, reversibility, and operational burden.\n- An approved or explicitly advisory architecture decision record that preserves decision owner, authority, rationale, rejected alternatives, conditions, dissent, and evidence that would change the outcome.\n- Logical, physical, deployment, runtime, integration, data, security, identity, privacy, governance, observability, operational, and user-control views as applicable.\n- Defined components, responsibilities, interfaces, contracts, states, dependencies, data handled, authority level, trust level, failure behavior, recovery behavior, ownership, and verification methods.\n- Versioned interface and data contracts with validation, compatibility, idempotency, timeout, retry, cancellation, reconciliation, error, audit, and evidence semantics.\n- Explicit separation between presentation, governed action, orchestration, domain logic, data, evidence, observability, and infrastructure boundaries.\n- Deployment, configuration, secrets, migration, rollback, support, maintenance, deprecation, and retirement architecture.\n- A solution assurance plan covering normal, boundary, failure, recovery, security, privacy, accessibility, performance, reliability, resilience, and owner-acceptance paths.\n- A durable implementation handoff that does not require engineers or agents to invent missing scope, interfaces, authority, evidence, or lifecycle behavior.","original_applicability":"- A product, application, service, platform feature, integration, device, AI capability, migration, modernization, or operational workflow requires a concrete design before implementation.\n- The solution crosses components, teams, repositories, data stores, networks, providers, environments, or trust boundaries.\n- Requirements include security, privacy, safety, accessibility, reliability, resilience, performance, support, compliance, recovery, or lifecycle obligations.\n- A solution must integrate with enterprise services, shared platforms, identity, data, policy, evidence, or observability.\n- Multiple implementation approaches are credible and tradeoffs must be made explicitly.\n- Existing systems are being extended, replaced, wrapped, migrated, decomposed, consolidated, or retired.\n- AI agents, models, tools, or autonomous functions can affect decisions, communications, data, users, or protected state.\n- Deployment, rollback, migration, support, and operation are material to success.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-04-01","REQ-04-02","REQ-04-03","REQ-04-04","REQ-05-01","REQ-05-02","REQ-05-03","REQ-05-04","REQ-06-01","REQ-06-02","REQ-06-03","REQ-06-04","REQ-07-01","REQ-07-02","REQ-07-03","REQ-07-04","REQ-07-05","REQ-08-01","REQ-08-02","REQ-08-03","REQ-08-04","REQ-19-01","REQ-19-02","REQ-19-03","REQ-19-04","REQ-19-05","REQ-20-01","REQ-20-02","REQ-20-03","REQ-20-04","REQ-21-01","REQ-21-02","REQ-21-03","REQ-21-04","REQ-21-05","REQ-29-01","REQ-29-02","REQ-29-03","REQ-30-01","REQ-30-02","REQ-30-03","REQ-30-04","REQ-31-01","REQ-31-02","REQ-31-03","REQ-31-04","REQ-31-05","REQ-32-01","REQ-32-02","REQ-32-03","REQ-32-04","REQ-34-01","REQ-34-02","REQ-34-03","REQ-34-04","REQ-35-01","REQ-35-02","REQ-35-03","REQ-36-01","REQ-36-02","REQ-36-03","REQ-36-04","REQ-37-01","REQ-37-02","REQ-37-03","REQ-37-04","REQ-38-01","REQ-38-02","REQ-38-03","REQ-38-04","REQ-38-05","REQ-39-01","REQ-39-02","REQ-39-03","REQ-39-04","REQ-40-01","REQ-40-02","REQ-40-03","REQ-41-01","REQ-41-02","REQ-41-03","REQ-41-04","REQ-42-01","REQ-42-02","REQ-42-03"],"decision_reason":"Complete 306-line original read. The assigned ground-up native, persistent, offline AI system materially changes responsibilities, authority, state, effects, recovery and lifecycle. It is not an exploratory artifact or a minor change covered by a verified architecture baseline.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_07.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/architecture_contract_reconciliation_20261006_07.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/023_SYSTEMS_ARCHITECTURE.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/023_SYSTEMS_ARCHITECTURE.txt","sha256":"389c9b97ea01b783b4bcbb8699f5492caa691b5e50524486cdeb8a4344c1cdac","bytes":32647,"section":"023","original_title":"SECTION 023 — Systems Architecture","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":3,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_03_Requirements_through_Hardware_Architecture.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A declared system-of-interest boundary with external actors, enabling systems, dependent systems, environments, jurisdictions, trust zones, lifecycle horizon, and excluded scope.\n- A set of architecture drivers traceable to requirements, risks, constraints, mission threads, owner journeys, operational concepts, enterprise principles, solution decisions, and lifecycle obligations.\n- Credible architecture alternatives evaluated against the same authorized criteria, mandatory constraints, evidence, assumptions, cost, complexity, human impact, reversibility, and operational burden.\n- A selected, conditionally selected, or advisory systems architecture with decision owner, authority state, rationale, dissent, conditions, rejected alternatives, and reopening triggers.\n- Logical, functional, behavioral, information, data, software, hardware, network, deployment, runtime, security, privacy, safety, reliability, resilience, observability, governance, and lifecycle views as applicable.\n- A decomposition into bounded system elements with purpose, responsibilities, inputs, outputs, state ownership, data handled, authority level, trust level, dependencies, interfaces, failure behavior, recovery behavior, operational owner, and verification method.\n- Explicit architectural invariants and forbidden states that cannot be traded away through local optimization or convenience.\n- Versioned interface contracts covering semantics, identifiers, protocol, timing, units, authentication, authorization, validation, compatibility, idempotency, timeout, retry, cancellation, reconciliation, errors, evidence, and audit behavior.\n- End-to-end mappings for critical owner, user, operator, safety, security, recovery, and evidence journeys across all participating elements.\n- Failure-domain, dependency, degradation, containment, recovery, rollback, backup, restore, and continuity architecture.\n- Deployment, configuration, secrets, environment, supply-chain, observability, support, maintenance, migration, modernization, deprecation, and retirement architecture.\n- An architecture-to-requirement and architecture-to-evidence trace that exposes unresolved allocations, unproven claims, and stale assumptions.\n- A durable handoff to specialist architecture and engineering sections without requiring downstream workers or agents to invent material structure, ownership, or authority.","original_applicability":"- The system contains multiple components, services, devices, models, agents, repositories, data stores, networks, facilities, suppliers, environments, or operators.\n- The system crosses trust, authority, identity, organizational, jurisdictional, physical, safety, security, privacy, or evidence boundaries.\n- Requirements must be allocated across interacting elements and cannot be proven by one component alone.\n- Critical behavior depends on sequencing, state transitions, concurrency, timing, consistency, availability, recovery, or external dependencies.\n- The system includes AI-assisted decisions, agent delegation, autonomous behavior, tool execution, or model-generated action proposals.\n- Existing architecture is being changed through migration, modernization, decomposition, consolidation, replacement, integration, or retirement.\n- Failure could cause material harm, loss, deception, privilege escalation, data corruption, evidence loss, operational blindness, or loss of owner control.\n- Architecture must support multiple environments or modes without weakening authority, evidence, or recovery boundaries.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-04-01","REQ-04-02","REQ-04-03","REQ-04-04","REQ-05-01","REQ-05-02","REQ-05-03","REQ-05-04","REQ-06-01","REQ-06-02","REQ-06-03","REQ-06-04","REQ-07-01","REQ-07-02","REQ-07-03","REQ-07-04","REQ-07-05","REQ-08-01","REQ-08-02","REQ-08-03","REQ-08-04","REQ-16-01","REQ-16-02","REQ-16-03","REQ-16-04","REQ-18-01","REQ-18-02","REQ-18-03","REQ-18-04","REQ-19-01","REQ-19-02","REQ-19-03","REQ-19-04","REQ-19-05","REQ-20-01","REQ-20-02","REQ-20-03","REQ-20-04","REQ-21-01","REQ-21-02","REQ-21-03","REQ-21-04","REQ-21-05","REQ-25-01","REQ-25-02","REQ-25-03","REQ-25-04","REQ-26-01","REQ-26-02","REQ-26-03","REQ-26-04","REQ-27-01","REQ-27-02","REQ-27-03","REQ-27-04","REQ-29-01","REQ-29-02","REQ-29-03","REQ-30-01","REQ-30-02","REQ-30-03","REQ-30-04","REQ-31-01","REQ-31-02","REQ-31-03","REQ-31-04","REQ-31-05","REQ-32-01","REQ-32-02","REQ-32-03","REQ-32-04","REQ-34-01","REQ-34-02","REQ-34-03","REQ-34-04","REQ-35-01","REQ-35-02","REQ-35-03","REQ-36-01","REQ-36-02","REQ-36-03","REQ-36-04","REQ-37-01","REQ-37-02","REQ-37-03","REQ-37-04","REQ-38-01","REQ-38-02","REQ-38-03","REQ-38-04","REQ-38-05","REQ-41-01","REQ-41-02","REQ-41-03","REQ-41-04","REQ-42-01","REQ-42-02","REQ-42-03"],"decision_reason":"Complete 332-line original read. Persistent cognition, protected authority, native UI, helpers, external effects and lifecycle require coherent system, state, trust, deployment and failure views. The 25 registered responsibilities do not automatically imply 25 processes or administrative independence.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_07.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/architecture_contract_reconciliation_20261006_07.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/024_DOMAIN_ARCHITECTURE.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/024_DOMAIN_ARCHITECTURE.txt","sha256":"9c587745d2a89f68a7fc53821c7b8aa65637e8ebabb9d7313444ed84b49ebdbd","bytes":30870,"section":"024","original_title":"SECTION 024 — Domain Architecture","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":3,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_03_Requirements_through_Hardware_Architecture.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A declared domain-architecture scope identifying the domain, enterprise and system boundaries, decision owner, authoritative experts and sources, intended consumers, jurisdictions, lifecycle horizon, and exclusions.\n- A domain landscape separating core, supporting, generic, external, regulatory, and enabling subdomains as applicable without assigning value labels unsupported by evidence.\n- A bounded-context map showing each context's purpose, language, model owner, authority, source of truth, rules, state, services, events, data, evidence, interfaces, dependencies, and lifecycle.\n- A context relationship map covering upstream and downstream responsibilities, published language, customer-supplier relationships, shared kernels or shared models, conformist dependencies, translation, anti-corruption layers, open-host services, and explicit isolation where applicable.\n- Canonical terminology within each bounded context and controlled translation where the same word has different meanings across contexts.\n- Domain models covering entities, value objects, aggregates or consistency boundaries where applicable, identifiers, classifications, policies, services, commands or intents, events, states, transitions, invariants, exceptions, time, and termination.\n- Explicit distinction between domain rule, organizational policy, legal obligation, workflow convention, software behavior, data representation, model inference, recommendation, and human decision.\n- Source-of-truth and authority ownership for every material domain fact, decision, state, rule, exception, and evidence object.\n- Versioned domain contracts for APIs, events, data exchange, decisions, evidence, and human or AI handoffs.\n- A change and evolution model covering effective dates, versioning, compatibility, migration, coexistence, deprecation, dispute, supersession, and retirement.\n- A domain-to-requirement, domain-to-system, domain-to-data, domain-to-interface, domain-to-policy, and domain-to-test traceability model.\n- A durable handoff to Business Architecture, Capability Architecture, Process Architecture, Functional Architecture, Operational Architecture, Information Architecture, Data Architecture, Application Architecture, Software Architecture, API Architecture, Event-Driven Architecture, AI Architecture, testing, operations, and lifecycle work.","original_applicability":"- The system spans multiple business, operational, scientific, legal, technical, organizational, or jurisdictional contexts.\n- Terms are overloaded, disputed, translated, or used differently by users, operators, systems, regulators, or data sources.\n- Multiple applications, services, teams, products, agents, models, or data stores represent the same real-world concepts.\n- Domain rules, state transitions, exceptions, approvals, evidence, or authority are material to correctness.\n- Integration, migration, modernization, merger, acquisition, platform consolidation, or data exchange requires semantic translation.\n- AI systems classify, summarize, recommend, generate, route, or act on domain information.\n- Domain errors could cause material harm, incorrect decisions, privacy or security violations, financial loss, safety failure, unfair treatment, or loss of accountability.\n- Domain meaning changes over time through policy, law, contract, product, process, organization, or lifecycle events.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-04-01","REQ-04-02","REQ-04-03","REQ-04-04","REQ-05-01","REQ-05-02","REQ-05-03","REQ-05-04","REQ-06-01","REQ-06-02","REQ-06-03","REQ-06-04","REQ-07-01","REQ-07-02","REQ-07-03","REQ-07-04","REQ-07-05","REQ-09-01","REQ-09-02","REQ-09-03","REQ-09-04","REQ-10-01","REQ-10-02","REQ-10-03","REQ-10-04","REQ-11-01","REQ-11-02","REQ-11-03","REQ-11-04","REQ-11-05","REQ-12-01","REQ-12-02","REQ-12-03","REQ-12-04","REQ-12-05","REQ-16-01","REQ-16-02","REQ-16-03","REQ-16-04","REQ-18-01","REQ-18-02","REQ-18-03","REQ-18-04","REQ-19-01","REQ-19-02","REQ-19-03","REQ-19-04","REQ-19-05","REQ-20-01","REQ-20-02","REQ-20-03","REQ-20-04","REQ-25-01","REQ-25-02","REQ-25-03","REQ-25-04","REQ-26-01","REQ-26-02","REQ-26-03","REQ-26-04","REQ-27-01","REQ-27-02","REQ-27-03","REQ-27-04","REQ-29-01","REQ-29-02","REQ-29-03","REQ-30-01","REQ-30-02","REQ-30-03","REQ-30-04","REQ-31-01","REQ-31-02","REQ-31-03","REQ-31-04","REQ-31-05","REQ-35-01","REQ-35-02","REQ-35-03"],"decision_reason":"Complete 320-line original read. Source 016 working meanings now require owned bounded contexts, explicit source hierarchy and translations preserving authority, state, provenance and lifecycle. A schema, database, class name or prompt represents meaning without proving it.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_07.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/architecture_contract_reconciliation_20261006_07.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/025_BUSINESS_ARCHITECTURE.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/025_BUSINESS_ARCHITECTURE.txt","sha256":"31df9b46daa7a23894b2a79c6d01e44ee7861b63874758a1492f9275d2b41eb6","bytes":37467,"section":"025","original_title":"SECTION 025 — Business Architecture","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":3,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_03_Requirements_through_Hardware_Architecture.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A declared enterprise or business boundary with included and excluded organizations, products, services, populations, jurisdictions, environments, partners, suppliers, channels, and lifecycle horizon.\n- A source-grounded current-state business architecture that distinguishes observed operation from policy, planned work, informal workaround, and inference.\n- A target-state business architecture traceable to mission, strategy, stakeholder outcomes, user evidence, domain meaning, requirements, risk, and authorized decisions.\n- Explicit value propositions and outcome statements identifying intended beneficiaries, affected parties, obligations, harms to avoid, evidence, assumptions, and success boundaries.\n- A value-stream architecture showing triggering need, participating stakeholders, capabilities, decisions, information, products, services, controls, evidence, support, recovery, and terminal outcome.\n- A business capability map with stable definitions, ownership, outcomes, dependencies, required competencies, resources, information, controls, measures, evidence, lifecycle state, and realization mechanisms.\n- An operating-model view defining how capabilities, organizations, roles, decision rights, processes, information, technology, partners, locations, and governance combine to deliver outcomes.\n- A decision-rights and accountability model that distinguishes authority, responsibility, consultation, execution, verification, approval, risk acceptance, and final human control.\n- A business information concept model identifying authoritative concepts, records, evidence, ownership, provenance, privacy, retention, and cross-domain translation needs.\n- A product, service, channel, customer, user, partner, supplier, and ecosystem view linked to capabilities and value streams.\n- A policy, rule, obligation, control, and exception view showing source, jurisdiction, owner, enforcement location, evidence, expiry, and escalation path.\n- Current, target, and transition states with dependencies, sequencing, investments, benefits, risks, adoption, training, migration, rollback, continuity, and retirement obligations.\n- A measurement architecture that distinguishes activity, output, outcome, benefit, harm, control effectiveness, resilience, owner operability, and evidence quality.\n- A durable handoff to Capability Architecture, Process Architecture, Functional Architecture, Operational Architecture, Information Architecture, organization design, product and service design, technology architecture, delivery, operations, and lifecycle governance.","original_applicability":"- Mission, business, product, portfolio, program, project, or transformation strategy must be translated into an operating structure.\n- The enterprise needs to understand how value is created across organizational, product, service, process, information, technology, partner, or jurisdiction boundaries.\n- Capabilities, value streams, roles, decision rights, policies, controls, products, services, or investments are unclear, duplicated, fragmented, or changing.\n- A merger, acquisition, divestiture, partnership, outsourcing, insourcing, reorganization, platform change, modernization, or retirement affects business operation.\n- A digital, automation, AI, or agent initiative could change authority, accountability, work allocation, user experience, evidence, or risk.\n- Business and technology teams disagree about what outcome is required, who owns it, or which operating constraint is binding.\n- Regulatory, contractual, privacy, safety, security, accessibility, records, or public-interest obligations must be embedded in operating design.\n- Portfolio and investment decisions require stable capability and value-stream context rather than project labels alone.\n- Current-state operations include manual workarounds, shadow systems, undocumented decisions, or hidden dependencies.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-00-01","REQ-00-02","REQ-00-03","REQ-00-04","REQ-00-05","REQ-03-01","REQ-03-02","REQ-03-03","REQ-03-04","REQ-04-01","REQ-04-02","REQ-04-03","REQ-04-04","REQ-09-01","REQ-09-02","REQ-09-03","REQ-09-04","REQ-13-01","REQ-13-02","REQ-13-03","REQ-13-04","REQ-13-05","REQ-17-01","REQ-17-02","REQ-17-03","REQ-19-01","REQ-19-02","REQ-19-03","REQ-19-04","REQ-19-05","REQ-20-01","REQ-20-02","REQ-20-03","REQ-20-04","REQ-22-01","REQ-22-02","REQ-22-03","REQ-22-04","REQ-23-01","REQ-23-02","REQ-23-03","REQ-23-04","REQ-24-01","REQ-24-02","REQ-24-03","REQ-24-04","REQ-24-05","REQ-29-01","REQ-29-02","REQ-29-03","REQ-30-01","REQ-30-02","REQ-30-03","REQ-30-04","REQ-31-01","REQ-31-02","REQ-31-03","REQ-31-04","REQ-31-05","REQ-32-01","REQ-32-02","REQ-32-03","REQ-32-04","REQ-34-01","REQ-34-02","REQ-34-03","REQ-34-04","REQ-35-01","REQ-35-02","REQ-35-03","REQ-38-01","REQ-38-02","REQ-38-03","REQ-38-04","REQ-38-05","REQ-39-01","REQ-39-02","REQ-39-03","REQ-39-04","REQ-41-01","REQ-41-02","REQ-41-03","REQ-41-04","REQ-42-01","REQ-42-02","REQ-42-03"],"decision_reason":"Complete 347-line original read. The product changes user experience, work allocation, AI participation, authority, evidence and recovery. Those bounded operating concerns apply even with one known decision owner. No commercial organization, workforce, customer population, service commitment or investment decision is inferred.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_07.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/architecture_contract_reconciliation_20261006_07.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/026_CAPABILITY_ARCHITECTURE.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/026_CAPABILITY_ARCHITECTURE.txt","sha256":"6ad0c83feb6acb6a25272fc46fcf5f5c87e21708a956a6375ce29657e9916789","bytes":36992,"section":"026","original_title":"SECTION 026 — Capability Architecture","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":3,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_03_Requirements_through_Hardware_Architecture.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A declared capability-architecture scope, authority, enterprise or system boundary, stakeholders, environments, jurisdictions, and lifecycle horizon.\n- A stable capability taxonomy and map that minimizes dependence on current organization, technology, provider, project, or process labels.\n- A capability catalog with purpose, outcome, beneficiary, owner, authority, scope, inputs, outputs, dependencies, competencies, information, process, functions, organization, technology, partners, controls, measures, evidence, resilience, support, and lifecycle state.\n- Capability levels or decomposition that preserve clear parent-child meaning and avoid arbitrary hierarchy.\n- Traceability from mission, strategy, value propositions, value streams, requirements, risks, obligations, products, services, and architecture decisions to capabilities.\n- A realization model showing how each capability is delivered through people, roles, skills, processes, functions, information, data, applications, technologies, facilities, suppliers, partners, AI, and governance.\n- A dependency and enabling-capability model exposing shared services, bottlenecks, concentration, common-cause failure, sequence, and evidence dependencies.\n- A current-state assessment that distinguishes documented existence, access, authorization, use, demonstration, performance, resilience, supportability, and effectiveness.\n- A target-state capability architecture with authorized priorities, required levels, performance measures, capacity, resilience, control, evidence, and lifecycle obligations.\n- A gap model separating absent capability, insufficient level, inadequate capacity, weak quality, missing authority, poor integration, unproven evidence, fragile dependency, unsupported operation, and retirement risk.\n- Capability increments and transition states with dependencies, investment, ownership, acceptance criteria, evidence gates, adoption, training, migration, rollback, continuity, and retirement.\n- A capability-to-initiative and capability-to-investment model that prevents project completion from being mistaken for capability delivery.\n- A capability measurement and assurance model that distinguishes readiness, availability, authority, performance, quality, resilience, user outcome, benefit, harm, control effectiveness, and evidence confidence.\n- A durable handoff to Process Architecture, Functional Architecture, Operational Architecture, Information Architecture, organization and workforce design, solution and technology design, portfolio and investment governance, testing, operations, and lifecycle management.","original_applicability":"- Strategy, Business Architecture, Enterprise Architecture, product strategy, portfolio, or transformation work requires a stable view of enduring abilities.\n- Investments, programs, projects, products, services, systems, platforms, teams, suppliers, or AI initiatives must be compared against common outcome requirements.\n- Capability ownership, dependency, maturity, capacity, resilience, evidence, or lifecycle state is unclear.\n- Multiple organizations or systems provide overlapping, fragmented, or duplicated capabilities.\n- A capability depends on cross-functional work, shared information, common platforms, external providers, specialized expertise, or distributed authority.\n- A merger, acquisition, divestiture, outsourcing, insourcing, reorganization, modernization, migration, or retirement changes how capability is realized.\n- A high-consequence capability requires explicit security, privacy, safety, accessibility, legal, resilience, recovery, continuity, or owner-control conditions.\n- AI or automation is proposed as a replacement or augmentation for human work, decision-making, analysis, operation, support, or control.\n- Portfolio and investment decisions require evidence of capability gaps and benefits rather than project advocacy.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-03-01","REQ-03-02","REQ-03-03","REQ-03-04","REQ-04-01","REQ-04-02","REQ-04-03","REQ-04-04","REQ-13-01","REQ-13-02","REQ-13-03","REQ-13-04","REQ-13-05","REQ-17-01","REQ-17-02","REQ-17-03","REQ-18-01","REQ-18-02","REQ-18-03","REQ-18-04","REQ-19-01","REQ-19-02","REQ-19-03","REQ-19-04","REQ-19-05","REQ-20-01","REQ-20-02","REQ-20-03","REQ-20-04","REQ-22-01","REQ-22-02","REQ-22-03","REQ-22-04","REQ-23-01","REQ-23-02","REQ-23-03","REQ-23-04","REQ-24-01","REQ-24-02","REQ-24-03","REQ-24-04","REQ-24-05","REQ-25-01","REQ-25-02","REQ-25-03","REQ-25-04","REQ-26-01","REQ-26-02","REQ-26-03","REQ-26-04","REQ-27-01","REQ-27-02","REQ-27-03","REQ-27-04","REQ-29-01","REQ-29-02","REQ-29-03","REQ-30-01","REQ-30-02","REQ-30-03","REQ-30-04","REQ-32-01","REQ-32-02","REQ-32-03","REQ-32-04","REQ-34-01","REQ-34-02","REQ-34-03","REQ-34-04","REQ-35-01","REQ-35-02","REQ-35-03","REQ-36-01","REQ-36-02","REQ-36-03","REQ-36-04","REQ-37-01","REQ-37-02","REQ-37-03","REQ-37-04","REQ-38-01","REQ-38-02","REQ-38-03","REQ-38-04","REQ-38-05","REQ-41-01","REQ-41-02","REQ-41-03","REQ-41-04"],"decision_reason":"Complete 344-line original read. The continuing intelligence product depends on enduring abilities realized through components, models, data, authority, controls and operations. A feature list, work-package list, downloaded asset or benchmark does not demonstrate operational capability.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_07.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/architecture_contract_reconciliation_20261006_07.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/027_PROCESS_ARCHITECTURE.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/027_PROCESS_ARCHITECTURE.txt","sha256":"b1da01a1954230b7d0ce1171dc7aa570ab6e5d2d1e7cb0be8dc67382e159ad35","bytes":41492,"section":"027","original_title":"SECTION 027 — Process Architecture","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":3,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_03_Requirements_through_Hardware_Architecture.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A declared process-architecture scope identifying the organization, system, value stream, product, service, capability, population, environment, jurisdiction, lifecycle horizon, and authority boundary covered.\n- A process taxonomy and catalog with stable identities, purposes, outcomes, triggers, owners, participants, boundaries, variants, states, dependencies, controls, evidence, measures, and lifecycle status.\n- A value-stream-to-process view connecting desired outcomes and stakeholder journeys to capabilities, processes, functions, information, decisions, products, services, and evidence.\n- Current-state process evidence that distinguishes observed behavior, documented procedure, policy expectation, informal workaround, proposed design, and unknown condition.\n- A target-state process architecture that preserves approved requirements, domain meaning, risk treatments, mandatory constraints, accessibility, security, privacy, safety, reliability, resilience, and owner-control obligations.\n- Process models showing start conditions, end conditions, activities, decisions, parallel work, synchronization, handoffs, waits, queues, exceptions, escalations, controls, evidence capture, and recovery paths as applicable.\n- Explicit process ownership and decision rights for each material step, including who may initiate, approve, execute, observe, verify, override, suspend, resume, or retire a process.\n- Process-to-function, process-to-capability, process-to-requirement, process-to-control, process-to-data, process-to-system, process-to-role, process-to-test, and process-to-evidence traceability.\n- A process variant model for environments, jurisdictions, user groups, operating modes, products, data classes, risk classes, and lifecycle stages without uncontrolled copying or drift.\n- A process performance and assurance model distinguishing activity completion, throughput, latency, quality, outcome, control effectiveness, user impact, resilience, recovery, and evidence freshness.\n- A process change, exception, escalation, rollback, migration, deprecation, and retirement model that can be handed to Functional Architecture, Operational Architecture, Information Architecture, delivery, testing, operations, maintenance, and lifecycle governance.","original_applicability":"- A capability, value stream, product, service, system, organization, or human-AI workflow requires multiple activities, actors, decisions, handoffs, states, controls, or lifecycle stages.\n- Work crosses organizational, system, domain, supplier, partner, jurisdictional, physical, or data boundaries.\n- Current work includes undocumented manual steps, queues, workarounds, approvals, escalations, exceptions, or shadow systems.\n- A change may alter who performs work, which decisions are made, what evidence is created, how users or operators recover, or where authority is exercised.\n- The process carries material security, privacy, safety, accessibility, legal, financial, ethical, reliability, resilience, support, maintenance, or retirement consequences.\n- Automation, AI, agents, orchestration, or workflow tools are proposed to recommend, decide, route, transform, approve, execute, monitor, or recover work.\n- Requirements or architecture must be allocated to end-to-end journeys and accountable process steps rather than to abstract components alone.\n- Performance, quality, control effectiveness, user outcome, operational readiness, or benefit cannot be understood from component metrics alone.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-07-01","REQ-07-02","REQ-07-03","REQ-07-04","REQ-07-05","REQ-08-01","REQ-08-02","REQ-08-03","REQ-08-04","REQ-09-01","REQ-09-02","REQ-09-03","REQ-09-04","REQ-10-01","REQ-10-02","REQ-10-03","REQ-10-04","REQ-11-01","REQ-11-02","REQ-11-03","REQ-11-04","REQ-11-05","REQ-12-01","REQ-12-02","REQ-12-03","REQ-12-04","REQ-12-05","REQ-14-01","REQ-14-02","REQ-14-03","REQ-15-01","REQ-15-02","REQ-15-03","REQ-16-01","REQ-16-02","REQ-16-03","REQ-16-04","REQ-17-01","REQ-17-02","REQ-17-03","REQ-18-01","REQ-18-02","REQ-18-03","REQ-18-04","REQ-19-01","REQ-19-02","REQ-19-03","REQ-19-04","REQ-19-05","REQ-20-01","REQ-20-02","REQ-20-03","REQ-20-04","REQ-21-01","REQ-21-02","REQ-21-03","REQ-21-04","REQ-21-05","REQ-24-01","REQ-24-02","REQ-24-03","REQ-24-04","REQ-24-05","REQ-25-01","REQ-25-02","REQ-25-03","REQ-25-04","REQ-26-01","REQ-26-02","REQ-26-03","REQ-26-04","REQ-29-01","REQ-29-02","REQ-29-03","REQ-30-01","REQ-30-02","REQ-30-03","REQ-30-04","REQ-31-01","REQ-31-02","REQ-31-03","REQ-31-04","REQ-31-05","REQ-32-01","REQ-32-02","REQ-32-03","REQ-32-04","REQ-34-01","REQ-34-02","REQ-34-03","REQ-34-04","REQ-35-01","REQ-35-02","REQ-35-03","REQ-36-01","REQ-36-02","REQ-36-03","REQ-36-04","REQ-37-01","REQ-37-02","REQ-37-03","REQ-37-04","REQ-39-01","REQ-39-02","REQ-39-03","REQ-39-04","REQ-41-01","REQ-41-02","REQ-41-03","REQ-41-04","REQ-42-01","REQ-42-02","REQ-42-03"],"decision_reason":"Complete 388-line original read. The observation-to-future-retrieval cycle crosses human, model, kernel, broker, observer, store and recovery responsibilities. Its ordered process, instance state, handoffs, waits and exceptions require explicit ownership and guard conditions.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_07.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/architecture_contract_reconciliation_20261006_07.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/028_FUNCTIONAL_ARCHITECTURE.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/028_FUNCTIONAL_ARCHITECTURE.txt","sha256":"151bdd6e80ceb5640547dfee77c82e1ae138740e73a87ea98aeb7c24465cc2ee","bytes":44233,"section":"028","original_title":"SECTION 028 — Functional Architecture","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":3,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_03_Requirements_through_Hardware_Architecture.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A declared functional-architecture scope identifying the system or organizational boundary, objectives, environments, users, operators, affected parties, lifecycle horizon, and authority.\n- A stable functional taxonomy and hierarchy with clear parent-child meaning, function identity, purpose, inputs, outputs, conditions, constraints, owner, allocation, dependencies, evidence, and lifecycle state.\n- A functional context model showing external actors, systems, services, people, devices, data, events, resources, commands, controls, authorities, trust boundaries, and evidence flows.\n- A functional decomposition that covers normal, boundary, denied, degraded, failure, recovery, maintenance, migration, deprecation, and retirement behavior where applicable.\n- Functional flows and behavior models connecting triggers, preconditions, transformations, decisions, outputs, states, timing, sequencing, concurrency, exceptions, and postconditions.\n- Explicit allocation of functions and interfaces to human roles, AI roles, system elements, components, services, devices, organizations, external systems, processes, or enabling systems.\n- Functional contracts defining inputs, outputs, units, schemas, identity, authority, timing, ordering, quality, error behavior, retry, idempotency, audit, evidence, and compatibility as applicable.\n- Traceability from objectives, domain meaning, requirements, capabilities, processes, risks, controls, functions, allocations, implementations, tests, evidence, acceptance, operations, maintenance, and retirement.\n- A functional-state model that distinguishes desired, defined, proposed, allocated, implemented, integrated, verified, validated, operational, degraded, blocked, superseded, and retired states.\n- A functional verification and validation basis that makes claims reproducible and distinguishes analysis or model evidence from implementation and observed-behavior evidence.\n- A durable handoff to Operational, Information, Data, Application, Software, Platform, Infrastructure, Integration, API, Event, Security, Privacy, Safety, Reliability, Resilience, Accessibility, Engineering, Testing, Operations, Maintenance, and Retirement work.","original_applicability":"- A system, service, product, organization, device, process, model, agent, or capability contains interacting behaviors, transformations, controls, decisions, or interfaces.\n- Requirements must be decomposed and allocated across people, functions, systems, components, services, devices, external providers, or lifecycle activities.\n- The system crosses software, hardware, data, human, organizational, domain, supplier, physical, or jurisdictional boundaries.\n- Functional behavior has material security, privacy, safety, accessibility, reliability, resilience, performance, support, maintenance, recovery, or retirement consequences.\n- Multiple implementations or architectural alternatives must satisfy the same outcome-level obligations.\n- AI, automation, orchestration, or external services may perform, recommend, constrain, or supervise a function.\n- Verification requires functional-level analysis, inspection, demonstration, simulation, testing, measurement, or traceability independent of implementation detail.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-04-01","REQ-04-02","REQ-04-03","REQ-04-04","REQ-05-01","REQ-05-02","REQ-05-03","REQ-05-04","REQ-06-01","REQ-06-02","REQ-06-03","REQ-06-04","REQ-07-01","REQ-07-02","REQ-07-03","REQ-07-04","REQ-07-05","REQ-08-01","REQ-08-02","REQ-08-03","REQ-08-04","REQ-09-01","REQ-09-02","REQ-09-03","REQ-09-04","REQ-10-01","REQ-10-02","REQ-10-03","REQ-10-04","REQ-11-01","REQ-11-02","REQ-11-03","REQ-11-04","REQ-11-05","REQ-12-01","REQ-12-02","REQ-12-03","REQ-12-04","REQ-12-05","REQ-13-01","REQ-13-02","REQ-13-03","REQ-13-04","REQ-13-05","REQ-14-01","REQ-14-02","REQ-14-03","REQ-15-01","REQ-15-02","REQ-15-03","REQ-16-01","REQ-16-02","REQ-16-03","REQ-16-04","REQ-17-01","REQ-17-02","REQ-17-03","REQ-18-01","REQ-18-02","REQ-18-03","REQ-18-04","REQ-19-01","REQ-19-02","REQ-19-03","REQ-19-04","REQ-19-05","REQ-20-01","REQ-20-02","REQ-20-03","REQ-20-04","REQ-21-01","REQ-21-02","REQ-21-03","REQ-21-04","REQ-21-05","REQ-22-01","REQ-22-02","REQ-22-03","REQ-22-04","REQ-23-01","REQ-23-02","REQ-23-03","REQ-23-04","REQ-24-01","REQ-24-02","REQ-24-03","REQ-24-04","REQ-24-05","REQ-25-01","REQ-25-02","REQ-25-03","REQ-25-04","REQ-26-01","REQ-26-02","REQ-26-03","REQ-26-04","REQ-27-01","REQ-27-02","REQ-27-03","REQ-27-04","REQ-28-01","REQ-28-02","REQ-28-03","REQ-29-01","REQ-29-02","REQ-29-03","REQ-30-01","REQ-30-02","REQ-30-03","REQ-30-04","REQ-31-01","REQ-31-02","REQ-31-03","REQ-31-04","REQ-31-05","REQ-32-01","REQ-32-02","REQ-32-03","REQ-32-04","REQ-33-01","REQ-33-02","REQ-33-03","REQ-34-01","REQ-34-02","REQ-34-03","REQ-34-04","REQ-35-01","REQ-35-02","REQ-35-03","REQ-36-01","REQ-36-02","REQ-36-03","REQ-36-04","REQ-37-01","REQ-37-02","REQ-37-03","REQ-37-04","REQ-38-01","REQ-38-02","REQ-38-03","REQ-38-04","REQ-38-05","REQ-41-01","REQ-41-02","REQ-41-03","REQ-41-04","REQ-42-01","REQ-42-02","REQ-42-03"],"decision_reason":"Complete 403-line original read. Material functions must be allocated independently of process sequence and implementation packaging. Cross-cutting authority, privacy, quality, accessibility, resilience and recovery cannot remain ownerless prose.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_07.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/architecture_contract_reconciliation_20261006_07.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/029_OPERATIONAL_ARCHITECTURE.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/029_OPERATIONAL_ARCHITECTURE.txt","sha256":"57ed6e065e3790f14c617fbaf0d0950c35fcbed5a19ebdb9de5dd6a009d1233e","bytes":20501,"section":"029","original_title":"SECTION 029 — Operational Architecture","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":3,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_03_Requirements_through_Hardware_Architecture.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A bounded operational concept with owners, users, operators, maintainers, support parties, environments, modes, dependencies, service boundaries, and lifecycle state.\n- A catalog of operational services, responsibilities, procedures, runbooks, controls, resources, records, evidence, and escalation paths.\n- Normal, boundary, emergency, degraded, maintenance, migration, recovery, and retirement operating models.\n- Explicit operational decision rights for start, stop, approve, change, suspend, restore, fail over, communicate, and retire actions.\n- Traceability from capabilities, processes, functions, requirements, risks, controls, systems, data, interfaces, tests, evidence, incidents, and owner acceptance.\n- Measurable readiness, service, outcome, control, reliability, resilience, support, and recovery conditions with truthful evidence states.","original_applicability":"- A product, service, system, capability, facility, process, or organization will be used by people or automated actors over time.\n- Operation requires roles, schedules, procedures, monitoring, support, resources, approvals, controls, or service commitments.\n- The system has production, field, offline, emergency, maintenance, degraded, multi-tenant, supplier, or jurisdictional operating modes.\n- Operational failure could affect safety, security, privacy, accessibility, reliability, resilience, rights, finances, service continuity, or public trust.\n- AI, automation, external providers, or long-running agents may act after design or deployment.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-07-01","REQ-07-02","REQ-07-03","REQ-07-04","REQ-07-05","REQ-08-01","REQ-08-02","REQ-08-03","REQ-08-04","REQ-10-01","REQ-10-02","REQ-10-03","REQ-10-04","REQ-19-01","REQ-19-02","REQ-19-03","REQ-19-04","REQ-19-05","REQ-20-01","REQ-20-02","REQ-20-03","REQ-20-04","REQ-21-01","REQ-21-02","REQ-21-03","REQ-21-04","REQ-21-05","REQ-22-01","REQ-22-02","REQ-22-03","REQ-22-04","REQ-23-01","REQ-23-02","REQ-23-03","REQ-23-04","REQ-24-01","REQ-24-02","REQ-24-03","REQ-24-04","REQ-24-05","REQ-25-01","REQ-25-02","REQ-25-03","REQ-25-04","REQ-26-01","REQ-26-02","REQ-26-03","REQ-26-04","REQ-27-01","REQ-27-02","REQ-27-03","REQ-27-04","REQ-28-01","REQ-28-02","REQ-28-03","REQ-29-01","REQ-29-02","REQ-29-03","REQ-30-01","REQ-30-02","REQ-30-03","REQ-30-04","REQ-31-01","REQ-31-02","REQ-31-03","REQ-31-04","REQ-31-05","REQ-32-01","REQ-32-02","REQ-32-03","REQ-32-04","REQ-34-01","REQ-34-02","REQ-34-03","REQ-34-04","REQ-35-01","REQ-35-02","REQ-35-03","REQ-36-01","REQ-36-02","REQ-36-03","REQ-36-04","REQ-37-01","REQ-37-02","REQ-37-03","REQ-37-04","REQ-38-01","REQ-38-02","REQ-38-03","REQ-38-04","REQ-38-05","REQ-39-01","REQ-39-02","REQ-39-03","REQ-39-04","REQ-40-01","REQ-40-02","REQ-40-03","REQ-41-01","REQ-41-02","REQ-41-03","REQ-41-04","REQ-42-01","REQ-42-02","REQ-42-03"],"decision_reason":"Complete 223-line original read. The continuing supervisor, leases, observation, work queues, protected effects, support, migration and endurance behavior must operate over time. Deployment or uptime alone cannot establish operational readiness.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_07.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/architecture_contract_reconciliation_20261006_07.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/030_INFORMATION_ARCHITECTURE.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/030_INFORMATION_ARCHITECTURE.txt","sha256":"e34d3401ba6c6fc9d3f1a9750746c3ffc3e554de4bc65591795ea6232b608f59","bytes":19118,"section":"030","original_title":"SECTION 030 — Information Architecture","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":3,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_03_Requirements_through_Hardware_Architecture.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A bounded information landscape with audiences, purposes, contexts, domains, owners, authorities, sensitivity, jurisdictions, and lifecycle.\n- Canonical terms, definitions, identifiers, taxonomies, ontologies, relationships, metadata, labels, and translation rules.\n- Information flows and views connecting users, operators, processes, functions, systems, data stores, interfaces, records, controls, and evidence.\n- Discoverability, navigation, search, filtering, accessibility, localization, presentation, and explanation structures appropriate to each audience.\n- Explicit ownership for meaning, quality, provenance, access, retention, correction, publication, and retirement.\n- Traceability from requirements, domain rules, processes, functions, data, applications, policies, controls, tests, evidence, and user outcomes.","original_applicability":"- People or systems must find, interpret, compare, exchange, update, publish, retain, or retire information.\n- Multiple domains, products, services, organizations, languages, jurisdictions, roles, or applications use related information.\n- Information affects decisions, rights, safety, privacy, security, accessibility, compliance, service, operations, or trust.\n- Search, navigation, recommendations, generated answers, dashboards, records, content, APIs, events, or knowledge systems are designed or changed.\n- Terms, schemas, metadata, provenance, access, retention, or presentation vary by context.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-06-01","REQ-06-02","REQ-06-03","REQ-06-04","REQ-09-01","REQ-09-02","REQ-09-03","REQ-09-04","REQ-10-01","REQ-10-02","REQ-10-03","REQ-10-04","REQ-11-01","REQ-11-02","REQ-11-03","REQ-11-04","REQ-11-05","REQ-12-01","REQ-12-02","REQ-12-03","REQ-12-04","REQ-12-05","REQ-14-01","REQ-14-02","REQ-14-03","REQ-24-01","REQ-24-02","REQ-24-03","REQ-24-04","REQ-24-05","REQ-29-01","REQ-29-02","REQ-29-03","REQ-31-01","REQ-31-02","REQ-31-03","REQ-31-04","REQ-31-05","REQ-32-01","REQ-32-02","REQ-32-03","REQ-32-04","REQ-33-01","REQ-33-02","REQ-33-03","REQ-35-01","REQ-35-02","REQ-35-03","REQ-36-01","REQ-36-02","REQ-36-03","REQ-36-04","REQ-40-01","REQ-40-02","REQ-40-03","REQ-41-01","REQ-41-02","REQ-41-03","REQ-41-04"],"decision_reason":"Complete 209-line original read. Context, memory, search, generated answers, evidence and native navigation require distinguishable source and derivative states and purpose-bound access. Discoverability, ranking and embeddings cannot create authority or truth.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_07.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/architecture_contract_reconciliation_20261006_07.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/031_DATA_ARCHITECTURE.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/031_DATA_ARCHITECTURE.txt","sha256":"2143aee856d982cfab3f9d912ecc8236785f1ce291fa1d89fbc818f1fadc1e6b","bytes":18962,"section":"031","original_title":"SECTION 031 — Data Architecture","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":3,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_03_Requirements_through_Hardware_Architecture.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A bounded data landscape with domains, entities, events, records, sources, consumers, owners, custodians, authorities, sensitivity, jurisdictions, and lifecycle.\n- Conceptual, logical, and physical data views with explicit mappings and intentional variation.\n- Data-flow, lineage, provenance, transformation, synchronization, replication, integration, and dependency models.\n- Data quality, validation, reconciliation, observability, access, privacy, security, resilience, backup, restore, retention, archival, migration, and deletion obligations.\n- Versioned data contracts and ownership for meaning, schema, quality, access, evidence, change, and retirement.\n- Traceability from objectives, requirements, domains, processes, functions, information, applications, controls, tests, evidence, decisions, and outcomes.","original_applicability":"- A system creates, receives, stores, transforms, exchanges, analyzes, publishes, generates, retains, archives, migrates, or deletes data.\n- Data influences identity, rights, safety, privacy, security, finance, compliance, operations, models, decisions, or user experience.\n- Multiple systems, domains, jurisdictions, environments, suppliers, or lifecycle stages share or derive data.\n- Data quality, freshness, lineage, availability, confidentiality, integrity, portability, retention, or deletion is material.\n- AI, analytics, search, recommendation, simulation, or automation uses data beyond a single bounded transient operation.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-06-01","REQ-06-02","REQ-06-03","REQ-06-04","REQ-07-01","REQ-07-02","REQ-07-03","REQ-07-04","REQ-07-05","REQ-10-01","REQ-10-02","REQ-10-03","REQ-10-04","REQ-11-01","REQ-11-02","REQ-11-03","REQ-11-04","REQ-11-05","REQ-12-01","REQ-12-02","REQ-12-03","REQ-12-04","REQ-12-05","REQ-22-01","REQ-22-02","REQ-22-03","REQ-22-04","REQ-23-01","REQ-23-02","REQ-23-03","REQ-23-04","REQ-24-01","REQ-24-02","REQ-24-03","REQ-24-04","REQ-24-05","REQ-25-01","REQ-25-02","REQ-25-03","REQ-25-04","REQ-26-01","REQ-26-02","REQ-26-03","REQ-26-04","REQ-27-01","REQ-27-02","REQ-27-03","REQ-27-04","REQ-29-01","REQ-29-02","REQ-29-03","REQ-30-01","REQ-30-02","REQ-30-03","REQ-30-04","REQ-31-01","REQ-31-02","REQ-31-03","REQ-31-04","REQ-31-05","REQ-35-01","REQ-35-02","REQ-35-03","REQ-36-01","REQ-36-02","REQ-36-03","REQ-36-04","REQ-38-01","REQ-38-02","REQ-38-03","REQ-38-04","REQ-38-05","REQ-40-01","REQ-40-02","REQ-40-03","REQ-41-01","REQ-41-02","REQ-41-03","REQ-41-04"],"decision_reason":"Complete 214-line original read. CETA receives, persists, transforms, retrieves, generates, exports, migrates and deletes consequential data. No small payload, table or model input is exempt merely because it is small. The exact data inventory, quality thresholds and physical migration behavior remain unfinished.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_07.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/architecture_contract_reconciliation_20261006_07.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/032_APPLICATION_ARCHITECTURE.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/032_APPLICATION_ARCHITECTURE.txt","sha256":"86bf5b0f8f6a189b50753b9777e137144be0692eb140bcd1ed6b39f31bb054da","bytes":20162,"section":"032","original_title":"SECTION 032 — Application Architecture","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":3,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_03_Requirements_through_Hardware_Architecture.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- An application landscape with stable identities, purposes, owners, users, environments, responsibilities, dependencies, criticality, data, interfaces, and lifecycle state.\n- Explicit application boundaries and allocation of functions, information, data, workflows, controls, and user journeys.\n- Application context, interaction, integration, state, deployment, configuration, observability, support, and recovery views.\n- Application contracts for interfaces, identity, authorization, data, events, timing, errors, compatibility, audit, and evidence.\n- Traceability from requirements, capabilities, processes, functions, information, data, security, privacy, operations, tests, evidence, and retirement.\n- A migration, coexistence, versioning, deprecation, support, and retirement model that preserves authority and continuity.","original_applicability":"- One or more applications, services, clients, jobs, agents, or user surfaces realize material functions or information journeys.\n- Application boundaries, integration, state, identity, data, user experience, deployment, support, or lifecycle affect outcomes.\n- Applications are composed, replaced, integrated, migrated, exposed to external users, or operated across environments.\n- Failure, unauthorized action, inaccessible behavior, data loss, dependency drift, or unsupported versions could cause material consequence.","original_non_applicability":null,"requirement_ids":["REQ-04-01","REQ-04-02","REQ-04-03","REQ-04-04","REQ-05-01","REQ-05-02","REQ-05-03","REQ-05-04","REQ-06-01","REQ-06-02","REQ-06-03","REQ-06-04","REQ-07-01","REQ-07-02","REQ-07-03","REQ-07-04","REQ-07-05","REQ-08-01","REQ-08-02","REQ-08-03","REQ-08-04","REQ-13-01","REQ-13-02","REQ-13-03","REQ-13-04","REQ-13-05","REQ-18-01","REQ-18-02","REQ-18-03","REQ-18-04","REQ-19-01","REQ-19-02","REQ-19-03","REQ-19-04","REQ-19-05","REQ-20-01","REQ-20-02","REQ-20-03","REQ-20-04","REQ-21-01","REQ-21-02","REQ-21-03","REQ-21-04","REQ-21-05","REQ-22-01","REQ-22-02","REQ-22-03","REQ-22-04","REQ-23-01","REQ-23-02","REQ-23-03","REQ-23-04","REQ-24-01","REQ-24-02","REQ-24-03","REQ-24-04","REQ-24-05","REQ-25-01","REQ-25-02","REQ-25-03","REQ-25-04","REQ-26-01","REQ-26-02","REQ-26-03","REQ-26-04","REQ-27-01","REQ-27-02","REQ-27-03","REQ-27-04","REQ-29-01","REQ-29-02","REQ-29-03","REQ-30-01","REQ-30-02","REQ-30-03","REQ-30-04","REQ-31-01","REQ-31-02","REQ-31-03","REQ-31-04","REQ-31-05","REQ-32-01","REQ-32-02","REQ-32-03","REQ-32-04","REQ-33-01","REQ-33-02","REQ-33-03","REQ-34-01","REQ-34-02","REQ-34-03","REQ-34-04","REQ-35-01","REQ-35-02","REQ-35-03","REQ-36-01","REQ-36-02","REQ-36-03","REQ-36-04","REQ-37-01","REQ-37-02","REQ-37-03","REQ-37-04","REQ-38-01","REQ-38-02","REQ-38-03","REQ-38-04","REQ-38-05","REQ-39-01","REQ-39-02","REQ-39-03","REQ-39-04","REQ-40-01","REQ-40-02","REQ-40-03","REQ-41-01","REQ-41-02","REQ-41-03","REQ-41-04","REQ-42-01","REQ-42-02","REQ-42-03"],"decision_reason":"Complete 216-line original read. The native client, trusted service, workers, models, custody, data stores, support and migration surfaces realize material responsibilities. A single executable is not exempt; a source tree, successful build or demo is not application acceptance.","inspection_evidence_refs":["handoff/source_reconciliation_20261006_07.md"],"bounded_working_artifact_refs":["handoff/architecture_contract_reconciliation_20261006_07.json"]},{"original_archive_path":"Thirstys Codex/03_SECTIONS/033_SOFTWARE_ARCHITECTURE.txt","path":"sources/codex/canonical_text/03_SECTIONS/033_SOFTWARE_ARCHITECTURE.txt","sha256":"08479920f17a22ce35c364d97c63d80f25967014182752535391e3e9ee3451bd","bytes":10601,"section":"033","original_title":"SECTION 033 — Software Architecture","original_status":"CONTENT-COMPLETE","applicability":"DIRECT","implementation_status":"IN_PROGRESS","conformance_status":"NOT_EVALUATED","reconciliation_instruction":"Read original applicability/prerequisites and map its applicable clauses to exact CETA requirements before coding the affected work package.","volume":3,"volume_pdf":"sources/codex/Thirstys_Codex_Standards_Edition_Volume_03_Requirements_through_Hardware_Architecture.pdf","original_required_outcome":"- A bounded software context with responsibilities, components, interfaces, data, control flow, state, dependencies, environments, owners, and lifecycle.\n- Versioned design views and contracts covering composition, quality attributes, security, observability, failure, recovery, compatibility, and change.\n- Traceability from requirements, functions, applications, data, interfaces, implementation, tests, evidence, operation, maintenance, and retirement.\n- Explicit human and AI participation, authority, tool access, review, fallback, and revocation where software 